<h2>India's single largest-value product export by our count</h2>
<p>Among every product we've verified trade data for, groundnut kernels represent the largest total export value — USD 823.2 million across roughly 730,152 MT in calendar 2024. And unlike many of our other product lines, this trade concentrates overwhelmingly in one region: Southeast Asia.</p>
<p>Indonesia alone imported USD 295.3 million, about 36% of the global total. Add Vietnam (USD 124.9 million), the Philippines (USD 70.3 million), Malaysia (USD 56.2 million) and Thailand (USD 53.9 million), and Southeast Asia accounts for roughly 73% of everything India exports in this category. If you're building a Southeast Asia sourcing relationship with India, groundnut kernels are arguably the single clearest anchor product available.</p>

<h2>Bold and Java: two different products</h2>
<p>India exports two main groundnut kernel types. Bold (runner-type) kernels are larger and are the more common export grade; Java (Spanish-type) kernels are smaller with a distinct flavour profile suited to different processing applications. Both are HPS (Hand-Picked-Selected) sorted to remove damaged, discoloured or foreign material, then graded by count per ounce, moisture and oil content. Tell us your intended use — snack food processing, confectionery, oil extraction, direct retail — and we'll recommend the appropriate type and grade.</p>

<h2>Sourcing regions and season</h2>
<p>We source from Gujarat's Saurashtra belt and Andhra Pradesh/Rajasthan, the two dominant Indian groundnut-producing regions. Kharif harvest runs October–December, and HPS processing (sorting, grading, hand-picking) continues through the following months from stored kharif stock — export volume from India is strongest November through March, which is worth factoring into your own ordering calendar if you're timing against Indonesian domestic harvest gaps.</p>

<h2>Indonesian import requirements</h2>
<p>Food products generally require an ML (Makanan Luar) registration number from BPOM (Badan Pengawas Obat dan Makanan), Indonesia's food and drug regulator, before they can legally be sold, plus an SKI (Surat Keterangan Impor) entry permit issued for each individual shipment. Both are the importer's process, supported by documentation we supply from our side — certificate of analysis, phytosanitary certificate and certificate of origin in particular. Halal certification is also frequently relevant given Indonesia's formalised system through BPJPH; your importer specifies the required certifying body and we match documentation accordingly.</p>

<h2>Why India competes here despite Southeast Asia's own production</h2>
<p>Indonesia, Vietnam and the Philippines all grow groundnut domestically, so Indian supply into this region reflects a genuine cost or volume advantage rather than a captive market. That's worth knowing as a buyer — it means Indian groundnut pricing is competing directly against regional alternatives, which keeps quotes honest on both sides.</p>

