<h2>What goes wrong on a first order, and how to avoid it</h2>
<p>Aflatoxin is the single most important operator-level issue on this product, and it deserves more than a passing mention. Aflatoxin is a mold-produced toxin that develops when groundnut is exposed to humidity and warm temperatures during storage, drying or transit — it is not an Indian-supply-specific risk, it's inherent to the crop worldwide, but it is exactly why testing discipline matters more here than on almost any other product we handle. A certificate of analysis states the aflatoxin level at the time of testing; the real risk is contamination developing after that test, during a humid transit or poor destination storage, which is why moisture control in packaging and container ventilation matters as much as the pre-shipment lab result itself. If a shipment fails an aflatoxin threshold on arrival, it is very rarely fraud — it's usually a storage or transit humidity issue, and it is a genuinely expensive problem to have, since destination-market rejection of a full container is a real possibility, not a theoretical one.</p>
<p>The second common friction point is grading disputes between Bold and Java kernels, or disputes within a count band — count-per-ounce is measured on a sample, and natural variation within a lot means a contract should specify a tolerance band, not a single exact number, or you're setting up a disagreement neither side can really win.</p>
<p>What first-time Indonesian buyers most often get wrong: assuming BPOM/SKI registration can be sorted out after the goods are already afloat. It can't, in practice — ML registration in particular needs lead time, and a shipment that arrives before your registration is in place either sits in demurrage-accruing storage or can't clear at all. Start your BPOM registration process well before you place your first order, not after you've already booked a vessel.</p>
